uestion that aimed to correct notation and keep the idea about top/bottom placement clear.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"strength_name\": \"Concrete visual models\",\n  \"strength_note\": \"You consistently used shapes, shaded parts, and everyday examples (the cake story) so students could count, see, and name fractions.\",\n  \"horizon_title\": \"Specific next-step feedback\",\n  \"horizon_note\": \"Giving short, concrete directions after answers—for example, telling a student exactly which number to write and why—would turn correct echoes into deeper understanding.\",\n  \"journey_note\": \"In this one lesson you reached 61% — you repeatedly used engaging visuals and warm examples that set a strong foundation.\",\n  \"score_framing\": \"Sixty-one percent is a clear waypoint, not a verdict: it shows strong habits and one concrete next skill to grow.\",\n  \"domain_whys\": {\n    \"high_leverage_practices\": \"This is developing because you frequently confirmed and praised student responses (for example, you affirmed students’ counts like '8 parts') — it's not full marks because you did not give specific, actionable guidance showing students what to do next after a mistake.\",\n    \"student_engagement\": \"This is strong because you drew students into counting, labeling, and everyday talk (for example, students counted parts and repeated labels like 'numerator') — it's not full marks because many responses remained short echoes and students did not give fuller explanations when prompted.\",\n    \"teacher_subject_knowledge\": \"This is developing because you used accurate visual models and named pieces of notation (for example, asking '9 we write above, and 10?') — it's not full marks because you did not highlight a named misconception or pose a multi-step problem that would require deeper student thinking.\"\n  }\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Summarizing assessment insights**\n\nThe FICO summary indicates that Lesson Plan Fidelity is the strongest area, though it wasn't